          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "No emergency pattern in the red-flag table matched this description. That is not a clearance: the table covers a fixed list of presentations, and a symptom it does not list can still be serious. If symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, seek medical advice.\n\nDifficulty sleeping for three months, at least three nights per week, along with daytime consequences, meets the definition of chronic insomnia. This is a condition where the difficulty sleeping persists even after any original cause might have resolved (condition-insomnia).\n\nThe presence of daytime consequences, such as fatigue, impaired attention, mood disturbance, or impaired performance, is an important part of defining chronic insomnia (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line).\n\nA clinician would want to assess your sleep patterns, possibly using a sleep diary. They would also screen for other conditions that commonly present as insomnia, such as obstructive sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, circadian rhythm disorders, depression, anxiety, pain, and the effects of substances or medications. Your clinician would also review your caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, and shift patterns (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line).\n\nIt is important to note that if poor sleep has no diagnosis recorded, it can be classified under symptoms in Chapter 18 of the ICD-10 (coding-insomnia-g47).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
